\title{The Factor-Local Exponent Plane and the Validity Edge of the Elliptic Curve Method}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{5 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Classical integer factoring algorithms applied to a semiprime $N=pq$ with $p\le q$ are
\emph{factor-local}: their cost is governed by the smaller prime $p$ rather than by $N$.
Fitting the ansatz $T \approx c\,p^{\alpha}$ across ensembles of semiprimes yields a
factor-local exponent plane, one coordinate per algorithm. A pooled measurement with
bootstrap confidence intervals over $6000$ semiprimes per experimental arm returned
$\alpha_{\mathrm{td}} = 1.0009\,[1.000,1.002]$ for trial division,
$\alpha_{\rho} = 0.4994\,[0.485,0.510]$ for the rho/birthday collision threshold, and
$\alpha_{\mathrm F} = 0.9932$ for Fermat's method, with trial division and rho first-order
invariant under changes of the conditional law of the second prime (the \emph{arm}) and
Fermat strongly non-invariant; the elliptic curve method admitted no single exponent, its
fit ranging over $[-0.86,+0.04]$.

We prove that each stable coordinate is the shadow of an exact identity and that the
fourth coordinate cannot exist. Trial division's cost on a semiprime equals $p$ exactly
and is exactly independent of $q$. The no-collision probability for $t$ uniform draws
from a set of size $m$ satisfies $1-\tfrac{t(t-1)}{2m}\le R(m,t)\le\exp(-\tfrac{t(t-1)}{2m})$,
pinning every constant-probability threshold to $[\sqrt m,\,1+\sqrt{2\log 2}\sqrt m]$.
Fermat's method halts at exactly $x=(p+q)/2$ and at no smaller abscissa, so its cost is
the arithmetic--geometric defect $(\sqrt q-\sqrt p)^2/2$, of order $p$ on bounded-ratio
arms, with $q$-derivative bounded below by $\tfrac12(1-1/\sqrt2)$ and with the exact
finite-$p$ fit $1+\log(\tfrac32-\sqrt2)/\log p$ on the arm $q=2p$. The exponents
$(1,1,\tfrac12)$ are rigid. For the elliptic curve method we prove a self-destruction
wall: once the stage-one bound exceeds the top of the Hasse window at both primes, every
curve degenerates simultaneously, the split event is empty, and the uncapped expected
time to a split is infinite. Total degeneration at $p$ occurs at bound $B$ if and only if
$B \ge B^{*}(p) := \max_{n\in \Wwin(p)} \mpp(n)$, the largest prime power in the Hasse
window, and $B^{*}(p)$ is least with this property. At $p=101$ the wall is $121=11^2$,
not the window's largest prime $113$. Degeneration is joint: one-sided crossing splits
deterministically, whence the validity edge $2B \le \min(p,q)$. Finally, the hypothesis
that the largest prime factor and the number of distinct prime factors of the order drive
stage-one firing is refuted by an explicit infinite family, and replaced by an exact
powersmoothness count across the width-$4\sqrt p$ window.
\end{abstract}

